Notes on the closer calls:

- **Unit 2/Unit 6**: multi-sentence blocks where the checker reported only one finding but the window actually contains more than two sentences fused together — I applied one-sentence-per-line to the whole block, then re-checked the resulting lines against the ~120-char limit, splitting at the first rightward (or, failing that, backward) clause boundary. Unit 6's remaining first line (164 chars) stays long because the only usable boundary (`, because`) was already spent.
- **Unit 3**: rejected every candidate that broke inside the backtick-quoted `` //go:debug `` span — splitting inside an inline code token corrupts it regardless of whether it counts as a "real" directive.
- **Unit 7**: rejected candidates that further split the second sentence (`Open already half-closed...`) at `when`/`so` — that sentence sits at ~126 chars but the checker did not raise a `long` finding on it, and it's a single sentence, so it stays whole.
- **Unit 8**: rejected `c01`, which fixes fused+wrap but leaves a resulting 135-char line unsplit despite a valid, both-sides-standalone `and` boundary at index 76 (found via backward scan since nothing qualifies rightward of 120). `c03` applies that split correctly.
